      "char_file": "홍조_illust.jpg",
      "body": ["Persistent redness, flushing,", "and burning on cheeks and nose", "", "It's often mistaken for", "acne or just blushing easily"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Cause: Sun Exposure Is the #1 Trigger",
      "char_file": "태양_illust.jpg",
      "body": ["81% of rosacea patients say", "sun exposure worsens flare-ups", "", "UV rays dilate and damage", "blood vessels under the skin"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Fix: Daily SPF 30+, Rain or Shine",
      "char_file": "태양_illust.jpg",
      "body": ["Apply broad-spectrum SPF 30+", "every morning, even indoors", "", "Reapply every 2 hours", "when you're outside"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Cause: Emotional Stress Triggers Flushing",
      "char_file": "긴장_illust.jpg",
      "body": ["79% of patients report", "stress as a trigger", "", "Stress hormones dilate vessels", "and trigger sudden flushing"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Fix: Build In Small Stress Breaks",
      "char_file": "긴장_illust.jpg",
      "body": ["Short breathing breaks,", "walks, or stretching help", "", "Even 5 minutes can lower", "your stress response"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Cause: Heat, Wind, and Hard Workouts",
      "char_file": "사우나_illust.jpg",
      "body": ["Hot weather (75%), wind (57%),", "and heavy exercise (56%)", "", "all raise skin temperature", "and dilate facial vessels"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Fix: Cool Down and Pace Your Workouts",
      "char_file": "사우나_illust.jpg",
      "body": ["Exercise in cooler hours", "and take breaks to cool down", "", "A cold compress after", "can calm flushing fast"]
